When passing string values to tools, send the content directly without escape sequences. For example, use real newlines in markdown content rather than literal backslash-n (\n) characters.

Link an already-uploaded Linear assetUrl to an existing issue as an attachment.

Use this only after:
1. prepare_attachment_upload returned an assetUrl and uploadRequest.
2. The client successfully PUT raw file bytes to uploadRequest.url.

This tool does not upload file content. It only creates the Linear attachment row.
If the direct upload failed or the signed URL expired, rerun prepare_attachment_upload and upload again.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__linear__create_attachment_from_upload(args: {
  // Linear upload assetUrl returned by prepare_attachment_upload
  assetUrl: string;
  // Issue ID or identifier (e.g., LIN-123). P-prefixed identifiers (e.g., P-ENG-123) are projects, not issues
  issue: string;
  // Optional attachment subtitle
  subtitle?: string;
  // Attachment title. Defaults to filename or asset URL
  title?: string;
}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```

When passing string values to tools, send the content directly without escape sequences. For example, use real newlines in markdown content rather than literal backslash-n (\n) characters.

Retrieve an issue attachment by ID. Use format=url to get its title and download URL without fetching the file content. Linear upload URLs are signed for five minutes; call again to refresh. External URLs are returned unchanged. Files embedded in comments have no issue attachment ID; use the URLs in list_comments attachments instead. Download and process documents with your own file tools.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__linear__get_attachment(args: {
  // Response format. Defaults to content.
  format?: "content" | "url";
  // Attachment ID
  id: string;
}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```

